  "id": "Compound::DB00455 - Gene::1565",
  "verbalization": "Loratadine, a second-generation antihistamine, exhibits a complex interaction with CYP2D6 through multiple mechanistic pathways. The evidence suggests that Loratadine binds to CYP2C19 and CYP2C8, which are both involved in the oxidation of arachidonic acid. This binding leads to the participation of these enzymes in the arachidonic acid epoxygenase and monooxygenase activities, which are ultimately linked to CYP2D6. These pathways highlight a potential role for Loratadine in modulating the metabolism of arachidonic acid, potentially influencing downstream inflammatory processes. The consistent scores across the three paths indicate a robust, albeit indirect, relationship between Loratadine and CYP2D6, mediated through the shared involvement of CYP2C family members in arachidonic acid metabolism. The mechanism underscores the importance of enzyme participation in the metabolic cascade, suggesting that Loratadine may exert its effects through a pathway involving the CYP2D6 enzyme.",
